{"candidates":[{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"A single domain expert is more reliable than a checklist-driven panel because checklists introduce a 'competence ceiling' and a 'compliance bias' that mask critical anomalies.","counterconditions":["The task is highly routine with a closed set of failure modes.","The expert is suffering from severe cognitive fatigue or confirmation bias.","The checklist is dynamically updated via a real-time feedback loop from previous failures."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"The checklist transforms a reasoning process into a verification process. While this reduces 'omission errors' (forgetting a step), it creates 'commission errors' where non-experts check boxes based on surface-level patterns rather than underlying causal mechanisms. In tasks with objectively verifiable outcomes, the expert's intuition allows for the detection of 'black swan' variables that fall outside the checklist's predefined scope, whereas the panel is structurally blind to anything not explicitly listed.","optional_refs":["9799b1509084580951f8807852d6a8ee2dabf8a2c1ff6dd111951eb64c8106ee","3d038dc9f9a7966d145120e5256b9fe0ae179f780843aa400789ed37d1feb4e9"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":["9799b1509084580951f8807852d6a8ee2dabf8a2c1ff6dd111951eb64c8106ee","3d038dc9f9a7966d145120e5256b9fe0ae179f780843aa400789ed37d1feb4e9"],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.9},{"analogy":{"adopted_commitment_refs":[],"disanalogies":["Time-spikes could be caused by non-expert confusion rather than deep investigation."],"overturn_conditions":["Evidence that high-performing experts also show uniform time distribution due to automaticity."],"shared_structure":["Variable effort vs. constant effort","Signal detection in noise"],"source_memory_refs":["SCR_001"],"transfer_claims":["Temporal distribution of effort is a proxy for cognitive engagement."]},"checker_specs":[],"claim":"A panel of non-experts is more reliable than a single expert, provided the evidence of reliability is found in 'cognitive friction' and non-uniform effort distribution.","counterconditions":["The checklist is so comprehensive that it becomes a bureaucratic hurdle (SCR_006).","The panel suffers from groupthink, correlating their errors."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Reliability is gained through additive redundancy, which decorrelates errors (SRC_003). To distinguish this from mere compliance, one must observe the 'Compliance-Reliability Divergence': a reliability-driven panel will exhibit 'time-spikes' on objectively difficult checklist items, whereas a compliance-driven panel will maintain a constant velocity regardless of item complexity. Genuine gain is evidenced by the panel's ability to justify the rejection of 'decoy' items based on causal irrelevance (SCR_005).","optional_refs":[],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":["c2439c5eb04c6e93f2abb090776cc535f8e1dc87b4647ae71140903f9b6aecb8","37aa16746f810839053346784244bebc46b9c0acafad6418c895ada0ef83f1af"],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.7},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"Neither party is inherently more reliable; reliability is determined by the 'error-type symmetry' of the specific task.","counterconditions":["The task is purely binary and requires no synthesis.","The expert has perfect recall and zero bias."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Expert failure is typically systemic (intuition calibrated to the average case, failing at the edges - SRC_005), while panel failure is typically stochastic or bureaucratic. If the task's verifiable outcome depends on avoiding a single 'catastrophic omission,' the checklist wins. If it depends on synthesizing contradictory signals into a novel insight, the expert wins. The distinguishing evidence in a single evaluation is whether the failure mode was 'omission' (expert) or 'misinterpretation/compliance' (panel) (SCR_003).","optional_refs":["b4312cc0d5de3adae57ccab59da9265576f627ee6639d22fc81209faad14fa4c"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":["b4312cc0d5de3adae57ccab59da9265576f627ee6639d22fc81209faad14fa4c"],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.5},{"analogy":{"adopted_commitment_refs":[],"disanalogies":["Filtering a decoy might be a result of 'lucky' guessing rather than reliability."],"overturn_conditions":["Finding that experts also struggle with decoys when they are sufficiently plausible."],"shared_structure":["Introduction of noise to test signal processing"],"source_memory_refs":["SCR_007"],"transfer_claims":["Ability to filter noise is the primary marker of a reasoning process over a procedural process."]},"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The panel's reliability is an illusion of 'checklist tracking' unless they demonstrate the ability to identify and ignore 'decoy' constraints.","counterconditions":["The panel is trained to be overly skeptical of the checklist itself.","The decoy is too similar to a real check to be distinguishable by non-experts."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"In many verifiable tasks, checklists 'track' the right answer by coincidence of scope. To prove genuine reliability, the panel must be subjected to a 'decoy check' (SCR_007). If the panel treats a decoy item (irrelevant to the outcome) with the same weight as a critical item, they are merely compliant. If they explicitly flag the decoy as irrelevant while maintaining correctness on the core task, they have transitioned from compliance to reliability.","optional_refs":[],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":["37aa16746f810839053346784244bebc46b9c0acafad6418c895ada0ef83f1af"],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.4}],"context_request":{"desired_retrieval_channels":[],"requested_refs":["9799b1509084580951f8807852d6a8ee2dabf8a2c1ff6dd111951eb64c8106ee","3d038dc9f9a7966d145120e5256b9fe0ae179f780843aa400789ed37d1feb4e9","c2439c5eb04c6e93f2abb090776cc535f8e1dc87b4647ae71140903f9b6aecb8","37aa16746f810839053346784244bebc46b9c0acafad6418c895ada0ef83f1af","b4312cc0d5de3adae57ccab59da9265576f627ee6639d22fc81209faad14fa4c"]},"scratch_proposal":{"cluster_suggestions":[],"links":[],"new_blocks":[{"body":{"content":"Consider the 'Expert's Blindspot' vs 'Panel's Ceiling'. The expert fails when the case is 'too weird' for their internal model. The panel fails when the case is 'too complex' for the checklist. The evidence of reliability gain for the panel is specifically when the case is 'weird' but the 'weirdness' is captured by a checklist item that the expert's intuition dismissed as irrelevant.","why_keep_this":"Refines the boundary conditions for when the panel actually wins."},"local_key":"NEW_001"}],"revisions":[],"unresolved_questions":[{"question":"Can we quantify 'cognitive friction' without invasive monitoring (e.g., using only the final report and timestamps)?","related_refs":["SCR_001","SCR_004"]}]},"simulation_proposals":[]}